\clearpage
\section{Rebuilding and checking this walkthrough}

From the Research folder, run
\begin{verbatim}
lake build GradientDescent
lake env lean GradientDescent/Check.lean
\end{verbatim}
The first command checks the umbrella module and its dependencies. The second
prints the logical axioms used by the main theorem, the unique-minimizer theorem,
and the two endpoint counterexamples. The existing audit reports only
\lean{propext}, \lean{Classical.choice}, and \lean{Quot.sound}, the standard
logical foundations used here.

The snippets in this document are extracted automatically from the Lean files
by \lean{build_exposition.py}. The annotation data contain the source-file name,
the inclusive line range, and the accompanying mathematical explanation.
The generator embeds the code into the standalone \lean{GradientDescent.tex};
compiling that file needs no external code include. The file
\lean{source_manifest.json} records the source hashes and the exact ranges
used, so the code-to-document correspondence can be checked mechanically.

To regenerate the walkthrough after a source or annotation change:
\begin{verbatim}
python3 GradientDescent/exposition/build_exposition.py
pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error \
  -output-directory=GradientDescent/exposition \
  GradientDescent/exposition/GradientDescent.tex
\end{verbatim}
Run the LaTeX command again to resolve the contents and references. If Lean
source lines move, update the affected annotation ranges before generating.

For a first pass, read the supporting-plane proof, the upper-gap proof,
\lean{gradient_descent_decrease}, and \lean{gradient_descent_gap_step}.
They show the key link between familiar inequalities and Lean commands.
Then read the geometric induction and final assembly to see how those local
inequalities produce the complete convergence theorem. The remaining sections
explain every supporting proof, including minimizer existence and the strict
step-size endpoints.
